I MIGRATED THE CALLERS MYSELF, which the spec put outside the arm's reach. Seven sites, and the
distinction between them is the interesting part:

  score.rs is the REAL one and now passes `scope.value().map(|sc| sc.departures.len())`. Because
  `scope` is a Measurement, an unassessed worktree arrives as None and yields Indeterminate --
  the capability is wired to its evidence, not merely present.

  eligible.rs, critique.rs and defects.rs pass Some(0), deliberately. These are synthetic
  observations that use `judge` as a boolean combinator, with no worktree and no scope to depart
  from. None would mean "not assessed" and turn a correct answer into a refusal to answer; in
  eligible.rs it would have made every arm ineligible. That these sites exist at all is the
  N-of-N finding from port-eligible, where my spec told every arm to route pass/fail through
  gate::judge and eligibility is not a run verdict. The comment at each site says so.

  defects.rs's verdict match gets an explicit OutOfScope arm rather than a wildcard. A wildcard
  absorbs the next variant silently, which is how a new state inherits an old one's behaviour.

